[
    {
        "content": "<p>Hi, I'm interested in formalizing the Boolean Decision Tree model of computation. I <a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover/cslib/pull/923\">created an initial PR</a> that formalizes complexity measures of boolean functions. </p>\n<p>References : <a href=\"https://homepages.cwi.nl/~rdewolf/publ/qc/dectree.pdf\">Buhrman-de Wolf Survey</a> and the Arora-Barak complexity theory textbook.</p>\n<p>Here is a <a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover/cslib/pull/925\">draft PR</a> defining the deterministic decision tree and its complexity. <br>\nThis is how the main definitions in the DecisionTree file look like right now. </p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">inductive</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">DecisionTree</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Nat</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kn\">where</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"bp\">|</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">leaf</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">output</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Bool</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">DecisionTree</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"bp\">|</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">node</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">i</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Fin</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">DecisionTree</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">DecisionTree</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">DecisionTree</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n</span>\n\n<span class=\"sd\">/-- Runs the input `x` through the decision tree and outputs the value at the leaf.</span>\n<span class=\"sd\">The left child is the `false` branch, the right child the `true` branch. -/</span>\n<span class=\"kn\">def</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">eval</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">DecisionTree</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Cube</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Bool</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"bp\">|</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">leaf</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">b</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">_</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=&gt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">b</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"bp\">|</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">node</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">i</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">l</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">r</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=&gt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">if</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">i</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">then</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">r</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">eval</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">else</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">l</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">eval</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">def</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">depth</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">DecisionTree</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ℕ</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"bp\">|</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">leaf</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">_</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=&gt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">0</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"bp\">|</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">node</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">_</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">l</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">r</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=&gt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">max</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">l</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">depth</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">r</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">depth</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">+</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">1</span>\n\n<span class=\"sd\">/-- Decision Tree t computes f if for all inputs evaluating x on t gives the</span>\n<span class=\"sd\">correct function value. -/</span>\n<span class=\"kn\">def</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Computes</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">t</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">DecisionTree</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">BoolFunc</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Prop</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"bp\">∀</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">t</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">eval</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span>\n\n<span class=\"sd\">/-- `D(f)`: the least depth over all decision trees computing f. -/</span>\n<span class=\"kn\">noncomputable</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kn\">def</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">complexity</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">BoolFunc</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ℕ</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">sInf</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">k</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">|</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∃</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">t</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">DecisionTree</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">t</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">Computes</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∧</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">t</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">depth</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">k</span><span class=\"o\">}</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>Looking for some opinions on the following : </p>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover/cslib/pull/890\">cslib#890</a> just got merged and it uses <code>abbrev BooleanFunction (n : ℕ) := (Fin n → Bool) → Bool</code> which is the same type as <code>BoolFunc</code> from my PR. Should these be unified to have some infra around BooleanFunctions where we define things like degree, composition, etc for boolean functions? Just a note,  for query complexity I use <code>{0,1}^n -&gt; {0,1}</code> which matches Arora-Barak and Buhrman-de Wolf. But Fourier Analysis side (O'Donnell) works with<code> {-1,1}^n -&gt; {-1,1}</code>. </li>\n<li>Complexity is noncomputable currently. Any suggestions for alternatives?</li>\n</ul>",
        "id": 625333881,
        "sender_full_name": "Vignesh Karri",
        "timestamp": 1789762479
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Hi Vignesh, nice to meet you here. Perhaps you could make the PR against <a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover/cslib/pull/685\">cslib#685</a> and use the query complexity framework from there for complexity purposes. It contains the essence of the query model that we discussed over email.</p>",
        "id": 625337309,
        "sender_full_name": "Shreyas Srinivas",
        "timestamp": 1789763526
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>About using {-1,1}, that’s basically because they are roots of unity. <span class=\"user-mention\" data-user-id=\"1034715\">@Sam Schlesinger</span> can help you with that. Normally your PR should adapt to existing definitions. but both the {0,1} and {-1,1} have their uses, so I guess if you define the mapping (<span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><msup><mn>1</mn><mi>b</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-1^b</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.9324em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">−</span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord\">1</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.8491em;\"><span style=\"top:-3.063em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\">b</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span>) from the former to the latter, using either should be fine.</p>",
        "id": 625338316,
        "sender_full_name": "Shreyas Srinivas",
        "timestamp": 1789763877
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Hey Shreyas, thanks for your reply!</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">inductive</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">BitQuery</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ℕ</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Type</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Type</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kn\">where</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"bp\">|</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">bit</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">i</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Fin</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">BitQuery</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Bool</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">def</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">bitModel</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Cube</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Model</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">BitQuery</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ℕ</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kn\">where</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">evalQuery</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">|</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">bit</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">i</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=&gt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">i</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">cost</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">_</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">1</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>For this query type and model a deterministic decision tree is just a <code>Prog (BitQuery n) Bool</code> and this is the same as the inductive definition I provided above. <br>\nI'm not entirely sure if we would use the extra generality provided by the query combinator approach for this model of computation. A decision tree requires only boolean branching and we don't require monadic effects here.</p>",
        "id": 625443151,
        "sender_full_name": "Vignesh Karri",
        "timestamp": 1789835879
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I don't think we need to make this PR against 685, I'd prefer to review it in its current form first.</p>",
        "id": 626361581,
        "sender_full_name": "Sam Schlesinger",
        "timestamp": 1790178376
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention\" data-user-id=\"1029365\">@Vignesh Karri</span> : I’ll defer to Sam and the maintainers of CSLib. One can argue that <a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover/cslib/pull/685\">cslib#685</a> isn’t merged yet. </p>\n<p>But in general this community follows the principle of not creating duplicate definitions. It’s not about whether full generality is necessary for a specific instance. If something can be expressed more abstractly or instantiated from an abstraction that’s preferred to creating new definitions. It leads to better tactic performance and a more coherent library, even it is harder to get accustomed to these general definitions in the beginning.</p>",
        "id": 626443868,
        "sender_full_name": "Shreyas Srinivas",
        "timestamp": 1790196360
    }
]